<!-- Debrief -->
<div class="siem-content blue-debrief" id="blue-debrief" style="display:none;flex:1;overflow-y:auto;">
  <div class="blue-debrief-label">Debrief — Signal in the Noise</div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Attack Summary</h3>
    <p>The attacker (<code>103.78.44.91</code>, Singapore) performed a targeted intrusion using stolen SSH credentials belonging to the sysadmin account <code>j.nakamura</code>. The attack followed a classic kill chain:</p>
    <p><strong>Reconnaissance</strong> (04:15) — DNS query for <code>fileserver.internal.corp</code> from an external IP, indicating the attacker already had knowledge of internal hostnames (likely from a prior breach or social engineering).</p>
    <p><strong>Initial Access</strong> (04:22) — Successful SSH login with valid credentials. No brute-force attempts, suggesting the credentials were obtained through phishing, credential stuffing, or a previous compromise.</p>
    <p><strong>Collection</strong> (04:25-04:28) — Multiple file accesses to <code>/srv/data/financial-reports/</code>, including Q4 earnings, M&A documents, and board materials.</p>
    <p><strong>Exfiltration</strong> (04:31) — 2.1 GB SCP transfer outbound to an external server at <code>45.77.132.18</code> in the Netherlands.</p>
    <p><strong>Cleanup</strong> (04:35) — SSH session terminated cleanly. Total dwell time: 13 minutes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Why this was hard to find</h3>
    <p>The attacker's activity was intentionally sparse — only 5 log entries across a 20-minute window, mixed in with 40+ entries of normal traffic. The key signals:</p>
    <p><strong>DNS anomaly:</strong> An external IP querying for an internal hostname is highly unusual. Internal DNS queries should come from internal IPs only.</p>
    <p><strong>Geographic anomaly:</strong> The sysadmin <code>j.nakamura</code> is based in Portland, OR. A login from Singapore at 04:22 UTC (8:22 PM SGT / 9:22 PM PDT) is suspicious but not impossible — without additional context, it could be legitimate travel.</p>
    <p><strong>Data volume:</strong> The 2.1 GB SCP transfer stands out in the firewall logs, but only if you correlate it with the SSH login from the same IP.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Detection improvements</h3>
    <p><strong>DNS monitoring:</strong> Alert on DNS queries for internal hostnames (<code>*.internal.corp</code>) originating from external IP addresses. This is a strong indicator of reconnaissance by an attacker who already has internal knowledge.</p>
    <p><strong>SSH hardening:</strong> Disable password authentication — require key-based auth or certificate-based auth. Implement IP allowlisting for SSH access. Deploy jump boxes / bastion hosts.</p>
    <p><strong>DLP rules:</strong> Alert on SCP/SFTP transfers exceeding 100 MB to external IPs. Require approval for transfers exceeding 500 MB.</p>
    <p><strong>UEBA baseline:</strong> Flag logins from new geolocations for privileged accounts. j.nakamura has never logged in from Singapore before — this should trigger an alert.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>Remediation</h3>
    <p><strong>Immediate:</strong> Reset j.nakamura's credentials. Revoke all SSH keys for the account. Block <code>103.78.44.91</code> and <code>45.77.132.18</code> at the perimeter firewall. Preserve forensic images of fileserver.</p>
    <p><strong>Short-term:</strong> Audit all SSH access logs for the past 90 days. Check if j.nakamura's credentials appear in any breach databases. Notify legal/compliance about potential financial data exfiltration.</p>
    <p><strong>Long-term:</strong> Implement network segmentation — financial data servers should not be directly accessible via SSH from the internet. Deploy a SIEM correlation rule that links DNS reconnaissance to subsequent SSH access. Require MFA for all remote access.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="blue-debrief-card">
    <h3>In the real world</h3>
    <p>This attack pattern mirrors the <strong>2020 SolarWinds</strong> intrusion, where attackers maintained a low profile by limiting their activity to short, targeted sessions. The average dwell time for an attacker in 2025 is <strong>10 days</strong> (CrowdStrike Global Threat Report), but sophisticated actors can complete their objectives in under an hour.</p>
    <p>Log correlation across multiple sources is the most effective detection technique. A single log source rarely tells the full story — it's the combination of DNS + Auth + Firewall that reveals the attacker's path through the network.</p>
  </div>
</div>

